## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure DP with prefix states, reconstruct decisions, and enforce a gap between segments — a common real-world pattern in budgeting or scheduling.

## Key Concepts
- Kadane variant to track best subarray ending at i
- 2D DP dp[i][t] for first i elements and t blocks
- Reconstructing endpoints with backpointers; gap handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const endSum = Array(n).fill(0);
  const endStart = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i === 0 || endSum[i-1] + A[i] < A[i]) {
      endSum[i] = A[i];
      endStart[i] = i;
    } else {
      endSum[i] = endSum[i-1] + A[i];
      endStart[i] = endStart[i-1];
    }
  }
  // dp and backtracking would follow (omitted for brevity)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you relax the gap constraint or support at-most-k blocks?
- How does the solution change with negative numbers or ties in endpoint choices?

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This problem explores non-overlapping palindromic substring selection using a two-dimensional DP that tracks prefix length and the number of selected substrings. It tests palindrome precomputation, transition logic, and solution reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Palindrome table**: pal[l][r] indicates whether s[l..r] is a palindrome
- **DP state**: dp[i][t] represents the maximum total length obtainable from prefix s[0..i] using exactly t substrings
- **Transition**: Either skip the current position or end a palindrome at i (starting at some l)
- **Reconstruction**: Use parent pointers to backtrack and retrieve the selected substrings

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalindromes(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pal[i][i] = true;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len - 1 < n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      if (s[l] === s[r] && (len === 2 || pal[l+1][r-1])) {
        pal[l][r] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1]?.[t] ?? 0;
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        if (pal[l][i]) {
          const prev = l > 0 ? dp[l-1][t-1] : 0;
          const val = prev + (i - l + 1);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            parent[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t-1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (i >= 0 && t > 0) {
    const choice = parent[i][t];
    if (!choice.skip) {
      result.unshift([choice.l, i]);
      t = choice.t;
      i = choice.l - 1;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxLength: dp[n-1][k], substrings: result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a hybrid DP: partitioning into exact segments plus inside-segment Kadane optimization; requires careful precomputation and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Range max-subarray precomputation (M[l][r])
- 2D DP over prefix length i and segments t
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// precompute M
for (let l=0; l<n; l++) {
  let maxEnd = 0, best = -Infinity;
  for (let r=l; r<n; r++) {
    maxEnd = Math.max(A[r], maxEnd + A[r]);
    best = Math.max(best, maxEnd);
    M[l][r] = best;
  }
}
```
```javascript
// DP
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let l=1; l<=i; l++) {
      const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + M[l-1][i-1];
