## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP with partitioning and additive segment costs; uses prefix sums to optimize the transition to O(nk).

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitioning into k segments
- Prefix sums to compute segment costs in O(1)
- State transition optimization with a running max and backpointers
- Reconstruction of cut points

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartitionScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  if (k <= 0 || k > n) return {score: 0, cuts: []};
  const diff = new Array(n-1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n-1; i++) diff[i] = Math.abs(A[i+1] - A[i]);
  const pref = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = pref[i-1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity, bestP = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const p = i - 1;
      const val = dp[p][t-1] - pref[p];
      if (val > best) { best = val; bestP = p; }
      dp[i][t] = pref[i-1] + best;
      take[i][t] = bestP;
    }
  }
  let cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = take[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = p;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests counting paths with variable step lengths using DP, a common interview pattern in operations and logistics contexts.

## Key Concepts
- 1D DP for path counting
- Handling variable jump lengths
- Modulo arithmetic for large counts

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(jumps){
  const n = jumps.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(n).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for(let i = 0; i < n; i++){
    const maxJump = Math.min(jumps[i], n - 1 - i);
    for(let s = 1; s <= maxJump; s++){
      dp[i + s] = (dp[i + s] + dp[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[n - 1] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(n) time if maxJump can be large but jumps form a sliding window of contributions?
- How would you adjust for different termination criteria or multiple destinations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space using prefix maxima per t-1?
- How would you extend to handle k up to n with space-saving tricks?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow at most k subarrays or to output the exact endpoints with a deterministic tie-breaker?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial DP with a non-additive cost function and reconstruction, plus a precomputation layer for inversions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix length i and blocks t with transition from a previous breakpoint j
- invCost(l,r) as block cost (inversions within subarray)
- Reconstruction pointers for block boundaries
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk) or O(nk) with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function precomputeInv(a){
  const n=a.length; const inv=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let cnt=0;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      for(let i=l;i<r;i++) if(a[i]>a[r]) cnt++;
      inv[l][r]=cnt;
    }
  }
  return inv;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize with divide-and-conquer DP or Knuth-like optimizations if invCost satisfies appropriate properties?
- How would you adapt to allowing at most k blocks or restoring the actual blocks efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a DP over partitioned subarrays with a non-standard per-subarray score and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on non-overlapping subarrays
- Prefix sums and range minimums
- Path reconstruction and base cases
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const P=[0]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) P[i+1]=P[i]+A[i];
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=P[i]-Math.min(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const minVal=Math.min(...A.slice(p,i));
        const val=dp[p][t-1]+(P[i]-P[p])-minVal;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; prev[i][t]=p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using prev
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize min queries? N log N via RMQ, or a deque per i.
- How to reconstruct the exact subarrays efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
